{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1772907487,"nonce":"+b6aVO+ka3IwSVbXzKnFdn9jz9c=","message":"**Debugging trajectory**\n\n**Hypothesis 1**: Route conflict — thought the new `GET /api/issues/find/:issueId` route might be intercepting `GET /api/issues` calls. Eliminated by checking the compiled routes/issues.js and confirming order is correct and regex anchors prevent ambiguity.\n\n**Hypothesis 2**: Runtime JS error in the new code — checked that `getIssueRepos`, `findIssue`, and `linked-issue-chip` all appear in the built app.js. Build was clean.\n\n**Hypothesis 3** (confirmed): Stale-while-revalidate race. After daemon restart, IssueScanner in-memory cache is cold. `GET /api/issues` returns `[]` while the async background scan runs. `app.tsx` fires `getIssueRepos()` on mount — this gets `[]` and dispatches `setIssueRepos([])`, which *overwrites* the `issueRepos` that was just restored from `loadCachedState()` (localStorage). No polling mechanism re-fetches it once the scan completes.\n\nConfirmed by: curling `/api/issues` right now returns 2 repos with 74+6 open issues — the backend is fine. The issue is a timing window that occurs only in the seconds immediately after restart.\n\n**Fix**: (a) Reducer guard in `state.ts`: don't overwrite non-empty `issueRepos` with empty. (b) Add `getIssueRepos()` to 10-second polling interval in `app.tsx` so it self-heals after the async scan completes.","files":null}]}